---
/**
 * Config reference — every field, as two browsable trees.
 *
 * This replaced two generated markdown pages ("Config reference" and
 * "design-system.json reference"), which overlapped: `themes.<name>` in the config
 * schema *is* a design system, so the config walk re-emitted the whole token
 * schema and the two pages documented the same fields twice.
 *
 * They are two trees rather than one for a reason that is easy to miss. The config
 * declares `designSystem.themes` as a LOOSER shape than `DesignSystemSchema` —
 * the full one is applied separately by `resolveTheme` — so its embedded copy is
 * an approximation, measurably missing the descriptions for `colors`,
 * `colors.palette`, `colors.roles` and `colors.utilities`. Rendering that copy as
 * the token reference would document the colour system less well than the
 * toolchain already does, silently. So the config tree is pruned at
 * `designSystem.themes` and the authoritative `jsonSchema` is rendered beside it.
 *
 * That mirrors the agent-facing split exactly: `vitops docs config` stops at the
 * wrapper and delegates the token fields to `vitops docs authoring`. Same two
 * documents, one page.
 *
 * Ids are dotted paths and don't collide: the config tree's roots are
 * `designSystem` / `organization` / `site`, the token tree's are `colors`,
 * `patterns`, … — and a bare `design-system.json` is a valid input, so
 * design-system-relative ids are the ones worth linking to.
 *
 * A standalone `.astro` route rather than a collection entry for the reason the
 * preview pages are: it renders live markup from the schema, which Markdown can
 * only express as hand-written HTML that drifts. Nav registration is the `PAGES`
 * list in `layouts/Docs.astro`.
 */
import { configJsonSchema, jsonSchema } from '@getvitops/generator';
import SchemaTree from '../../components/SchemaTree.astro';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';

const title = 'Config reference';
const description =
  'Every field of the three-section config — designSystem, organization and site — plus the full design-token schema. Browsable, filterable, and generated from the published JSON Schema.';
---

<Docs title={title} description={description} generated={true}>
  <p>
    Generated from <code>config.schema.json</code> and <code>schema.json</code>, the same schemas
    <code>vitops validate</code> checks against — so they always match validation. Every field is
    linkable by its dotted path: <code>#site.analytics.clarityId</code>, <code>#colors.palette</code>.
  </p>
  <p class="font-footnote">
    For what belongs in each section and when you need a config at all, start with
    <a class="link" href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}guides/config`.replace(/\/\/+/g, '/')}
      >Your config</a
    >. Agents should read <code>vitops docs config</code> and <code>vitops docs authoring</code>
    instead of this page.
  </p>

  <h2>Project config</h2>
  <p>
    The three sections of a project config. Token fields live under <code>designSystem</code> and are
    listed in full below, so this tree stops at the wrapper.
  </p>
  <SchemaTree
    schema={configJsonSchema}
    prune={['designSystem.themes']}
    label="Filter config fields"
    ariaLabel="Project config fields"
  />

  <h2>Design tokens</h2>
  <p>
    Every field of a design system — what <code>designSystem.themes.&lt;name&gt;</code> holds, and
    what a standalone <code>design-system.json</code> contains on its own.
  </p>
  <SchemaTree
    schema={jsonSchema}
    label="Filter token fields"
    ariaLabel="Design token fields"
  />
</Docs>
